# Node
node_modules/
.next/
out/
next-env.d.ts
*.tsbuildinfo

# Auth.js and database secrets. NEVER COMMITTED.
#
# `AUTH_SECRET`, `AUTH_GITHUB_ID` and `AUTH_GITHUB_SECRET` are real credentials:
# the OAuth client secret can mint tokens against somebody's repositories, and
# `AUTH_SECRET` signs the session cookies -- leaking it lets anyone forge a
# session, which on this application means approving a security gate.
#
# The repository root's .gitignore already covers `.env`; these are the Next.js
# variants it does not match.
.env*.local
.env.development
.env.production

# ── A NEGATION, AND IT IS LOAD-BEARING ───────────────────────────────────────
#
# The repository root's `.gitignore:27` holds a bare `runs/`, for the pipeline's run
# logs and paused state (~10k gitignored files). A bare pattern with a trailing slash
# matches a directory AT ANY DEPTH -- so it also swallowed `web/app/api/runs/`, which
# is the run list and run detail endpoints.
#
# MEASURED, and this is why the negation is here rather than a rename: `git add -A web`
# reported success and staged nothing for those two routes, and `git status` showed a
# clean tree. `git check-ignore -v` named the cause:
#
#     .gitignore:27:runs/   web/app/api/runs/route.ts
#     .gitignore:27:runs/   web/app/api/runs/[runId]/route.ts
#
# Two endpoints Lane J is building against would have been absent from the branch with
# every gate green -- `tsc`, `eslint` and `vitest` all read the WORKING TREE, so none
# of them can see a file that is not committed. That is this repository's signature
# failure shape (every check reporting success while the thing did not happen) arriving
# through a gitignore pattern.
#
# The alternative was renaming the route, which would change the public URL from
# `/api/runs` to something else to work around a pattern meant for a different
# directory. The URL is the contract Lane J imports; the ignore file is not.
!app/api/runs/
